Yes, you can use static and final together in Java. When used together, they define a constant that is shared among all instances of the class and cannot be changed once it is initialized. This combination is commonly used to define constants in Java.

Key Points:
Class-level Constant: The value is associated with the class itself rather than any instance.
Shared Across Instances: All instances of the class share the same value.
Immutable: The value cannot be changed once it is assigned.
Access: Can be accessed using the class name directly.
Example
Here’s an example demonstrating the use of static final together:

java
Copy code
public class Constants {
    // Define static final variables (constants)
    public static final int MAX_VALUE = 100;
    public static final String GREETING = "Hello, World!";
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Accessing static final variables using the class name
        System.out.println(Constants.MAX_VALUE);   // Output: 100
        System.out.println(Constants.GREETING);    // Output: Hello, World!
        
        // Trying to change the value will cause a compilation error
        // Constants.MAX_VALUE = 200; // Error: cannot assign a value to final variable MAX_VALUE
    }
}
Explanation:
Declaration: public static final int MAX_VALUE = 100;

static: The variable belongs to the class, not to any instance.
final: The variable's value cannot be changed once it is initialized.
int MAX_VALUE = 100;: Initializes the variable.
Access: In the main method, the constants are accessed using the class name Constants.

Benefits:
Consistency: Ensures that the constant value remains the same throughout the application.
Memory Efficiency: Since the value is shared among all instances, it saves memory.
Readability: Makes the code more readable and maintainable by clearly indicating that the value is a constant.
Use Cases:
Mathematical Constants: public static final double PI = 3.14159;
Configuration Constants: public static final int MAX_USERS = 10;
Common Strings: public static final String ERROR_MESSAGE = "An error occurred.";
Summary:
Using static final together is a common practice in Java to define constants. It provides a way to create values that are class-level, shared among all instances, and immutable. This is useful for defining configuration values, fixed values, and constants used throughout the application.

