The final keyword in Java, when applied to variables, means that once the variable is assigned a value, it cannot be changed. Whether final belongs to an object or a class depends on whether it is combined with static.

Final Instance Variables
When a final variable is an instance variable (i.e., it is not static), it belongs to the instance (or object) of the class. Each object of the class can have a different value for the final variable, but once assigned, the value cannot be changed.

Example:

java
Copy code
public class Example {
    final int instanceVariable;

    Example(int value) {
        this.instanceVariable = value; // Must be initialized, can be different for each object
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Example obj1 = new Example(10);
        Example obj2 = new Example(20);

        System.out.println(obj1.instanceVariable); // Output: 10
        System.out.println(obj2.instanceVariable); // Output: 20
    }
}
Final Static Variables
When a final variable is also static, it belongs to the class. This means there is only one copy of the variable, shared among all instances of the class. The value is constant and cannot be changed after initialization.

Example:

java
Copy code
public class Example {
    static final int classVariable = 100; // Initialized at declaration

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Accessing static final variable using class name
        System.out.println(Example.classVariable); // Output: 100

        // The following line would cause a compilation error
        // Example.classVariable = 200;
    }
}
Key Points
Final Instance Variables: Each instance of the class can have a different value, but once assigned, the value cannot be changed.
Final Static Variables: There is a single value shared among all instances of the class, and this value cannot be changed.
Summary
Keyword Combination	Description
final	Applied to instance variables. Each object can have a different value, but the value is immutable once set.
static final	Applied to class variables. The value is shared among all instances and cannot be changed once set.
Example Demonstrating Both
java
Copy code
public class Example {
    final int instanceVariable; // Final instance variable
    static final int classVariable = 100; // Final static variable

    Example(int value) {
        this.instanceVariable = value; // Must be initialized in constructor
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Example obj1 = new Example(10);
        Example obj2 = new Example(20);

        // Instance variable
        System.out.println(obj1.instanceVariable); // Output: 10
        System.out.println(obj2.instanceVariable); // Output: 20

        // Static final variable
        System.out.println(Example.classVariable); // Output: 100
    }
}
In this example:

instanceVariable is a final instance variable, and its value is unique to each object.
classVariable is a final static variable, and its value is the same across all instances of the class.






